{
  "semantics": {
    "attributes": {
      "final": {},
      "header": {}
    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "It is not sufficient merely to tell you to annotate your classes with the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Transactional"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " annotation, add "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@EnableTransactionManagement"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " to your configuration, and expect you to understand how it all works. To provide a deeper understanding, this section explains the inner workings of the Spring Framework’s declarative transaction infrastructure in the context of transaction-related issues."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The most important concepts to grasp with regard to the Spring Framework’s declarative transaction support are that this support is enabled "
          },
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "via AOP proxies"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "xref",
            "target": "core/aop/proxying.html#aop-understanding-aop-proxies"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " and that the transactional advice is driven by metadata (currently XML- or annotation-based). The combination of AOP with transactional metadata yields an AOP proxy that uses a "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "TransactionInterceptor"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " in conjunction with an appropriate "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "TransactionManager"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " implementation to drive transactions around method invocations."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "admonition",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Spring AOP is covered in "
          },
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "the AOP section"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "xref",
            "target": "core/aop.html"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null,
        "type": "note"
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Spring Framework’s "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "TransactionInterceptor"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " provides transaction management for imperative and reactive programming models. The interceptor detects the desired flavor of transaction management by inspecting the method return type. Methods returning a reactive type such as "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Publisher"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " or Kotlin "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Flow"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " (or a subtype of those) qualify for reactive transaction management. All other return types including "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "void"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " use the code path for imperative transaction management."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Transaction management flavors impact which transaction manager is required. Imperative transactions require a "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "PlatformTransactionManager"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ", while reactive transactions use "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "ReactiveTransactionManager"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " implementations."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "@Transactional"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " commonly works with thread-bound transactions managed by "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "PlatformTransactionManager"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ", exposing a transaction to all data access operations within the current execution thread. Note: This does "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "not"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "emphasis",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " propagate to newly started threads within the method."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "A reactive transaction managed by "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "ReactiveTransactionManager"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " uses the Reactor context instead of thread-local attributes. As a consequence, all participating data access operations need to execute within the same Reactor context in the same reactive pipeline."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "When configured with a "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "ReactiveTransactionManager"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ", all transaction-demarcated methods are expected to return a reactive pipeline. Void methods or regular return types need to be associated with a regular "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "PlatformTransactionManager"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ", for example, through the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "transactionManager"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " attribute of the corresponding "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "@Transactional"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " declarations."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          }
        ],
        "context": "admonition",
        "id": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null,
        "type": "note"
      },
